## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formalize DP for partitioning into constrained blocks and compare top-down vs bottom-up implementations with proper modulo handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with pruning
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Positive number optimization
- Modulo arithmetic and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function countPartitions(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n) return 1;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];

    let sum = 0, ways = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum > target) break;
      if (sum === target) {
        ways = (ways + dfs(j + 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    return memo[i] = ways;
  }

  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulation sketch
function countPartitionsBottomUp(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n && sum + nums[j] <= target; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum === target) {
        dp[j+1] = (dp[j+1] + dp[i]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Empty input: return 0
- Large target: handle with modulo and pruning
- Single element equal to target: return 1
- No valid partition: return 0

## Correctness Justification
Top-down explores all valid partitions by recursively trying all possible block endings. Memoization avoids recomputation. Bottom-up builds from base case, accumulating ways to reach each position. Both respect positive numbers enabling early termination when sum exceeds target.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if costs were a non-linear function cost(s) instead of s? 
- How would you extend to count the number of ways to reach n with min cost, not just the cost itself?

