## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement non-recursive mergesort on a linked list, a common real-world pattern where recursion is costly or prohibited. Tests pointer manipulation, stability of merge, and correctness of bottom-up passes, plus O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space.

## Key Concepts

- Bottom-up mergesort
- In-place linked-list merge
- Stability
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function mergeTwo(l1, l2) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null };
  let tail = dummy;
  while (l1 && l2) {
    if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
      tail.next = l1;
      l1 = l1.next;
    } else {
      tail.next = l2;
      l2 = l2.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = l1 || l2;
  return dummy.next;
}

function sortList(head) {
  if (!head || !head.next) return head;
  // length
  let len = 0, p = head;
  while (p) { len++; p = p.next; }
  const dummy = { next: head };
  for (let w = 1; w < len; w <<= 1) {
    let prev = dummy;
    let curr = dummy.next;
    while (curr) {
      // split left
      let left = curr;
      let i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const right = curr.next;
      curr.next = null;
      // split right
      curr = right;
      i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const next = curr ? curr.next : null;
      if (curr) curr.next = null;
      const merged = mergeTwo(left, right);
      prev.next = merged;
      while (prev.next) prev = prev.next;
      curr = next;
    }
  }
  return dummy.next;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to a doubly linked list?
- How does this compare to a recursive mergesort in terms of stack usage?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine topo sort with DP and counting paths; ensures handling of unreachable nodes and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting
- DP on DAG
- Shortest path counting modulo
- Edge relaxation and correctness sketch

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathsInDAG(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges) { adj[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++; }
  const topo = [];
  const q = [];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  for (let i=0;i<q.length;i++) {
    const u = q[i]; topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
  }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  dist[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for (const [v,w] of adj[u]) {
      const nd = dist[u] + w;
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; }
      else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD; }
    }
  }
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if edges can have negative weights but the graph remains a DAG? 
- How to extend to output the K smallest distances with counts efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a compact prefix-search data structure and implement tie-breaking logic using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequencies stored at each node
- Prefix search via traversal to the prefix end node
- DFS collection of all words in the subtree
- Sorting by frequency (descending), then lexicographic order for ties

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Autocomplete {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  addWord(word, freq) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.freq = freq;
  }

  getSuggestions(prefix, limit) {
    if (limit <= 0) return [];

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (currentNode, path) => {
      if (currentNode.freq > 0) {
        results.push({ word: path, freq: currentNode.freq });
      }
      for (const [char, childNode] of currentNode.children) {
        dfs(childNode, path + char);
      }
    };

    dfs(node, prefix);

    results.sort((a, b) => {
      if (b.freq !== a.freq) return b.freq - a.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });

    return results.slice(0, limit).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```
